    "redesign_cost_faq": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "row_1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does jewelry redesign cost?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Most projects land between $150 and $6,000, depending on what you're making. A ring-to-pendant conversion runs $150&ndash;$600. Redesigning a ring with your own stones is typically $1,500&ndash;$3,000. A custom engagement ring is $2,500&ndash;$6,000. Every redesign is custom, so the cost depends on the design, materials, and labor involved &mdash; and because you supply your own gems and gold, you usually save significantly compared with buying new. After a free consultation you receive a transparent, itemized quote before any work begins. These are real ranges based on typical projects, not a binding quote &mdash; what moves the final number is the condition of your stones, the finished weight of the metal, and how intricate the design becomes. I confirm all of it with you, in writing, before any work begins.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to reset a diamond?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$150&ndash;$400 if you already have the setting and I'm only doing the setting work. $500&ndash;$1,800 if the setting is included &mdash; that covers a ready-made setting plus the labor. If you'd like a setting designed from scratch around your stone, that's $1,500&ndash;$3,500. Resetting is one of the most affordable redesigns because your centre stone is already yours &mdash; you're paying for the setting and the setting work, not a new diamond. These are typical ranges rather than a binding quote; I confirm the exact figure with you in writing before any work begins.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What affects the cost of a jewelry redesign?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>The main factors are design complexity, how much new gold or platinum is needed beyond what you supply, whether we reuse your own stones or source new ones, the number of stones and setting style, and any finishing such as engraving. As a rough guide: new 14k gold adds $200&ndash;$500, 18k adds $300&ndash;$700, and platinum adds $500&ndash;$1,100. A few new accent stones add $200&ndash;$800; sourcing a new centre stone adds $1,500&ndash;$6,000.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Does using my own gold and stones make a redesign cheaper?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Yes. I responsibly refine gold or platinum you no longer wear and apply its value toward your new piece, and reusing your own diamonds and gemstones means you don't pay for new stones &mdash; often the single biggest cost in fine jewelry. I don't take any percentage of the refining payout; the full value belongs to you.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to redesign an engagement ring?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$1,500&ndash;$3,000 when we reuse your existing diamond and gold &mdash; far less than commissioning a new ring from scratch. If you'd like an entirely new setting designed around your stone, that's $1,500&ndash;$3,500. A custom engagement ring made from scratch is $2,500&ndash;$6,000.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to remake a wedding band?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$600&ndash;$1,800, and it's one of the most budget-friendly projects &mdash; especially when your original gold is refined and reused. The cost depends on the new design and the finished weight of the metal.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_7": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to turn a ring into a necklace?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$150&ndash;$600 for a straightforward conversion that keeps the ring's character. If you'd prefer a new pendant made from your gold rather than a conversion, that's $900&ndash;$2,200. Reusing your own stone and gold keeps it affordable while preserving the sentiment.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_8": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to combine several pieces into one?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$2,000&ndash;$4,000. This is some of the most meaningful work I do &mdash; several heirlooms brought together into a single new piece &mdash; and also the most involved, which is what the range reflects.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_9": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How do I get a price for my jewelry redesign?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Book a free consultation and send photos of your pieces. I create a design and a transparent, itemized quote before any work begins &mdash; nothing proceeds until you approve it.</h5>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "row_ring": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to redesign a ring?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$1,500&ndash;$3,000 for most rings, when we reuse the stones and gold you already have. A simpler change &mdash; resetting a stone into a plain new band, say &mdash; can be less; an intricate setting with several stones can be more. Because you already own the two most expensive parts, redesigning almost always costs less than buying the equivalent piece new. You get an itemised quote after a free consultation, before any work begins.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_repurpose": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to repurpose jewelry?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>It depends on what you're making rather than what you're starting with. Turning a ring into a pendant runs $150&ndash;$600. Reworking a ring into a new ring is typically $1,500&ndash;$3,000. Bringing several pieces together into one is $2,000&ndash;$4,000. If your old pieces contain gold you don't want to keep, refining it puts that value straight toward the new piece &mdash; and I take no percentage of it.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_newfromold": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to make a new ring from an old one?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>$1,500&ndash;$3,000 in most cases. I unset your stones, refine or reuse the metal, and build the new ring around what you already own. If there's more gold than the new piece needs, the surplus is refined and credited back to you &mdash; occasionally that covers a good part of the cost.</h5>"
          }
        }
      },
